CONCLUSIONS
Once the conventional friction factor equations have been validated with air and water in the smooth channel configuration, air and water are tested in the aligned and offset roughness configuration in minichannels with hydraulic diameters ranging from 769μm to 1.82mm. The following
conclusions are drawn from the present work:
1. Smooth rectangular minichannels with relative roughness of ε/Dh<0.000375 and hydraulic diameters of 325μm≤Dh≤1819μm show no signs of early
transition to turbulent flow, thus validating conventional flow transition of Re=2300. 2. The experimental laminar friction factor is valid in calculating the friction factor in rectangular
minichannels, and should be calculated using the constricted hydraulic diameter, Dh,cf .
3. Aligned and offset surface roughness microstructures show different relative roughness values. 4. As the relative roughness decreases, the critical Reynolds number increases for a machine roughened surface. The early transition is caused by the effects of the microstructure roughness and can be modeled
as Rec = 118.67(ε/dh,cf) -0.7885.
